**Accepted Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Department of Defense Releases its 2022 Strategic Reviews - National Defense Strategy, Nuclear Posture Review, and Missile Defense Review","publisher":"U.S. Department of Defense","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://www.defense.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/3201683/department-of-defense-releases-its-2022-strategic-reviews-national-defense-stra/?idU=1","claim":"The 2022 NPR reaffirmed deterrence as the fundamental role of U.S. nuclear weapons and limited use to extreme circumstances.","locator":"line 218","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official release of the administration's strategic reviews.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden as commander-in-chief through administration nuclear policy.","contrary_evidence":["The posture keeps a robust deterrent and does not adopt sole purpose or no-first-use."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. removes over 30 kilograms of highly enriched uranium from Japan","publisher":"Department of Energy, National Nuclear Security Administration","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://www.energy.gov/nnsa/articles/us-removes-over-30-kilograms-highly-enriched-uranium-japan","claim":"Biden and Kishida announced removal of HEU from three Japanese sites, reducing weapons-usable material.","locator":"lines 172-176","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official nonproliferation action with direct presidential attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's nonproliferation diplomacy with Japan.","contrary_evidence":["This is a discrete risk-reduction success, not a broad disarmament settlement."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.6","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on the Tenth Review Conference for the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-tenth-review-conference-for-the-treaty-the-non-proliferation-nuclear-weapons","claim":"Biden said his administration prioritized reducing the role of nuclear weapons and was ready to negotiate a new arms-control framework to replace New START.","locator":"lines 62-70","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Presidential statement reproduced from official release text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's public nuclear policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["The same statement preserves deterrence and links progress to Russia's cooperation."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement by the United Nations Security Council Permanent Members on Preventing Nuclear War and Avoiding Arms Races","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-01-03","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/joint-statement-the-united-nations-security-council-permanent-members-preventing-nuclear","claim":"The P5 statement affirmed that nuclear war cannot be won, called nuclear weapons defensive, and committed to strengthen measures against unauthorized or unintended use.","locator":"lines 63-69","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential archive of a joint nuclear-risk statement involving the United States.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to Biden's 2022 posture.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as part of Biden's joined P5 nuclear stance.","contrary_evidence":["It is a joint statement, so attribution is shared with other nuclear powers."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.3","1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks to the United Nations General Assembly in New York City","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-the-united-nations-general-assembly-new-york-city-17","claim":"Biden publicly condemned Putin's overt nuclear threats, urged diplomacy on nonproliferation, and said a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.","locator":"lines 65, 133-134","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous presidential speech text preserved in the official archive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's own rhetoric and crisis signaling.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetorical condemnation is not the same as a documented de-escalation mechanism."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout of President Biden's Meeting with President Andrzej Duda of Poland","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/readout-president-bidens-meeting-with-president-andrzej-duda-poland","claim":"Biden reaffirmed the U.S. commitment to Article 5 and NATO ally security while discussing support for Ukraine and reduced dependence on Russian energy.","locator":"line 63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential readout reproduced in the archive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the Ukraine crisis in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's alliance signaling and crisis management.","contrary_evidence":["Alliance reassurance is indirect evidence for nuclear responsibility rather than a direct command-and-control act."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks With President Andrzej Duda of Poland in a Briefing on Humanitarian Efforts for Ukraine in Jasionka, Poland","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-with-president-andrzej-duda-poland-briefing-humanitarian-efforts-for-ukraine","claim":"Biden said the central task was keeping democracies united to curb the devastation caused by Putin's war.","locator":"line 64","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous presidential remarks reproduced in the official archive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's crisis diplomacy.","contrary_evidence":["Humanitarian and coalition messaging does not by itself prove de-escalation."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on International Assistance Efforts for Ukraine in Warsaw, Poland","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-international-assistance-efforts-for-ukraine-warsaw-poland","claim":"Biden framed the war as a struggle against brutality and repression and linked U.S. support to enduring resistance to the invasion.","locator":"line 63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official archive transcript of public remarks in Warsaw.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's wartime public messaging.","contrary_evidence":["The passage is rhetorical and does not itself show a specific nuclear risk decision."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks Prior to a Meeting With President Andrzej Duda of Poland in Warsaw, Poland","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-prior-meeting-with-president-andrzej-duda-poland-warsaw-poland","claim":"Biden recalled his long-standing NATO commitment to Poland and repeated the for your freedom and for ours framing.","locator":"line 88","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official archive transcript of Biden's Warsaw remarks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's alliance and deterrence signaling.","contrary_evidence":["This is alliance rhetoric, not a direct arms-control or safeguard action."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on the National Security Strategy","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-national-security-strategy","claim":"Biden said Russia's nuclear threats endanger the nonproliferation regime and described his administration as reinvigorating alliances while managing great-power competition responsibly.","locator":"lines 62-75","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential statement tied to the 2022 NSS release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's strategic framing and policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["The statement emphasizes competition and deterrence, not disarmament alone."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Under Secretary Jenkins' Remarks at the U.S. Press Conference on U.S. Goals and Priorities for the IAEA General Conference","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/under-secretary-jenkins-remarks-at-the-u-s-press-conference-on-u-s-goals-and-priorities-for-the-iaea-general-conference/","claim":"The U.S. said it would work with the IAEA on AUKUS safeguards and maintain open and transparent engagement on nonproliferation standards.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official administration remarks, but the page was inaccessible directly and only the search snippet was available here.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Late 2022 context for dual-use and safeguards policy.","ruler_attribution":"Administration-level policy under Biden, though not a direct Biden speech.","contrary_evidence":["AUKUS also raised proliferation concerns among critics."],"lenses":["1B.6","1B.7","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ending Nuclear Test Explosions","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/our-mission/ending-nuclear-tests","claim":"The CTBT bans nuclear weapon test explosions everywhere and is a core part of the nuclear arms control and disarmament framework.","locator":"lines 226-236","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative treaty-body explanation of the treaty's purpose and verification logic.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"General treaty context relevant to Biden's 2022 nonproliferation posture.","ruler_attribution":"Context only, not a direct Biden act.","contrary_evidence":["This source explains treaty context and does not attribute a specific Biden decision."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/information-materials/frequently-asked-questions","claim":"The CTBTO notes that the United States signed but has not ratified the CTBT and that voluntary moratoriums can exist even without ratification.","locator":"lines 381-385, 376-381","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative treaty-body explanation of treaty status and moratorium norms.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Relevant structural background for 2022 U.S. test-ban posture.","ruler_attribution":"Context only, not a direct Biden act.","contrary_evidence":["Non-ratification limits the strength of any claim of full legal commitment."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"10. World nuclear forces","publisher":"SIPRI","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.sipri.org/yearbook/2022/10","claim":"SIPRI reported that the United States and Russia continued modernizing their nuclear arsenals and that global reductions of operational warheads had stalled.","locator":"lines 88-101","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent technical assessment by a leading research institute using open-source estimates.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 inherited baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited strategic baseline and comparison context for Biden.","contrary_evidence":["This is country-level baseline data, not a direct judgment on Biden's own decisions."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Global nuclear arsenals are expected to grow as states continue to modernize - New SIPRI Yearbook out now","publisher":"SIPRI","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.sipri.org/media/press-release/2022/global-nuclear-arsenals-are-expected-grow-states-continue-modernize-new-sipri-yearbook-out-now","claim":"SIPRI estimated that nearly 2000 warheads were kept in high operational alert, nearly all belonging to Russia or the USA, and said modernization would likely increase arsenals over the next decade.","locator":"lines 73-80","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press release summarizing the institute's annual nuclear-forces assessment.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 baseline and inherited posture context.","ruler_attribution":"Context only, not direct Biden conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Modernization baseline does not itself prove deterioration or improvement under Biden."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden's Disappointing Nuclear Posture Review","publisher":"Arms Control Association","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2022-12/focus/bidens-disappointing-nuclear-posture-review","claim":"ACA argued the 2022 NPR preserved a dangerous reliance on the threat of nuclear use and missed opportunities to reduce nuclear risk.","locator":"lines 79-85","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent expert critique written after the NPR release and directly focused on the policy text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden's final 2022 posture text.","contrary_evidence":["The critique also acknowledges some movement toward arms control and risk reduction."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Biden Nuclear Posture Review: Obstacles to Reducing Reliance on Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"Arms Control Association","publication_date":"2022-01","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2022-01/features/biden-nuclear-posture-review-obstacles-reducing-reliance-nuclear-weapons","claim":"ACA said Biden entered office intending to narrow the nuclear role, but institutional resistance made that goal difficult to realize cleanly.","locator":"lines 107-112","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent analysis of the policy process and its constraints, but pre-finalization.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early 2022 policy-process context.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden's intended policy and the constraints around it.","contrary_evidence":["This is a forecast and process analysis, not an outcome audit."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The 2022 Nuclear Posture Review: Arms Control Subdued By Military Rivalry","publisher":"Federation of American Scientists","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://fas.org/publication/2022-nuclear-posture-review/","claim":"FAS said the 2022 NPR resisted pressure to add new nuclear weapons and instead adjusted the existing force posture while integrating conventional and nuclear planning.","locator":"line 49","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent nuclear-policy analysis by a leading technical source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden's administration and the NPR it released.","contrary_evidence":["The same analysis notes continuing military rivalry and not a decisive break from deterrence-heavy policy."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"How would Trump and Biden handle US nuclear policy upon reelection?","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2024-07-08","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/how-would-trump-and-biden-handle-us-nuclear-policy-upon-reelection/","claim":"Brookings said Biden had been comparatively cautious on nuclear policy, prioritized stability with other nuclear powers, and strengthened assurances to allies.","locator":"lines 165-168, 185-195","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent analytical assessment, but written retrospectively in 2024.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective over the Biden period, useful for synthesis but not primary evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden's nuclear-policy approach.","contrary_evidence":["The same analysis says he maintained a robust deterrent rather than a purely arms-control posture."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.6","1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"U.S. has sent private warnings to Russia against using a nuclear weapon","publisher":"The Washington Post","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2022/09/22/russia-nuclear-threat-us-options/","claim":"The Biden administration had been sending private warnings to Moscow for months about grave consequences if Russia used a nuclear weapon in Ukraine, while keeping the response deliberately vague.","locator":"lines 33-36","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting based on U.S. officials on a live crisis issue.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's crisis-management approach through administration channels.","contrary_evidence":["The source relies on unnamed officials and does not disclose the private message content."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden's 'Armageddon' warning wasn't based on new intelligence, US says","publisher":"The Guardian","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/07/biden-armageddon-warning-russia-nuclear-weapons-ukraine","claim":"The White House said Biden's Armageddon warning was not based on new intelligence and did not require a change in U.S. nuclear posture.","locator":"lines 155-163","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting that directly quotes the White House response to Biden's remarks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's rhetoric and the administration's response.","contrary_evidence":["The remark was unusually stark and drew allied caution."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden denounces Putin's nuclear threats as reckless in UN address","publisher":"The Guardian","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/21/joe-biden-putin-nuclear-weapons-ukraine-un-general-assembly","claim":"At the UN, Biden denounced Putin's nuclear threats as reckless and said Russia's actions violated the UN Charter and nonproliferation responsibilities.","locator":"lines 162-163, 207-212","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous major-outlet reporting on a major presidential speech, with direct quotations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's public nuclear-risk messaging.","contrary_evidence":["The passage is rhetorical and does not itself prove operational de-escalation."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President-elect Biden Announces Key Nominations for the Department of State","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2021-01-16","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/press-release-president-elect-biden-announces-key-nominations-for-the-department-state","claim":"Biden nominated Bonnie Jenkins, a nuclear proliferation expert and veteran of nuclear security summits, to be Under Secretary for Arms Control and International Security Affairs.","locator":"lines 63-78, 102-107","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official transition release listing nuclear-security appointments and credentials.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited staffing baseline that shaped 2022 decision-making.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's expert staffing choices.","contrary_evidence":["Appointment quality does not by itself prove better outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Reading the Nuclear Tea Leaves: Policy and Posture in the Biden Administration","publisher":"CSIS","publication_date":"2021-2022","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/reading-nuclear-tea-leaves-policy-and-posture-biden-administration","claim":"CSIS argued the Biden administration would need integrated deterrence and integrated arms control to reduce nuclear risk effectively.","locator":"lines 323-330, 332","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized independent analysis of the Biden nuclear-policy process, though partly prospective.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early Biden context and process framing.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden's nuclear-policy approach and constraints.","contrary_evidence":["This is analytic projection rather than a 2022 outcome audit."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A New Nuclear Age?","publisher":"CSIS","publication_date":"2024-2025","url":"https://www.csis.org/analysis/new-nuclear-age","claim":"CSIS said the 2022 NPR did not adopt sole purpose but did retire some capabilities and emphasized that deterrence and risk reduction must coexist.","locator":"lines 252-252, 291-301","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent synthesis that is later and broader than 2022, but still useful for retrospective calibration.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective over the Biden period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Biden-era policy in hindsight.","contrary_evidence":["Retrospective synthesis is less direct than contemporaneous 2022 evidence."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}

**Search Ledger**
- Candidates found: about 58
- Documents opened: 30
- Duplicate or syndicated copies collapsed: 14
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items discarded: 9
- Inaccessible or technical-error items: 7
- Accepted distinct URLs: 21

**Material Gaps**
- Direct official White House or State Department pages on the 2022 NPT review and AUKUS safeguards were partly inaccessible here; I used the archive or secondary corroboration where needed.
- Stronger direct evidence on internal command-and-control safety, testing posture, and named expert-staffing decisions during 2022 would improve 1B.3 and 1B.7.
- 1B.9 remains the thinnest lens; current support is mostly indirect from AUKUS safeguards, CTBT/CTBTO context, and broader nonproliferation framing.
- The strongest crisis evidence is about rhetoric and private warnings, not a declassified operational backchannel record.

**Next Best Directions**
- Search the IAEA, CTBTO, and State Department archive for 2022 AUKUS safeguards, NPT, and test-ban materials.
- Look for declassified or contemporaneous records on U.S.-Russia backchannel communication and deconfliction during the Ukraine nuclear scare.
- Add more independent policy-analysis coverage from specialist outlets if you want a denser 1B.3 / 1B.7 / 1B.9 record.
